#show: doc => note_template([Structure Learning], doc)

#title()

When we have a dataset, and so observations on data, we might want to build a
model (whatever we choose) only by looking at the data. This basically means to
build a structure of conditional dependences among variables, in order to run a
learning algorithm in learn the parameters of each random variable distribution.

In *structure learning* we work under the assumption that a *true model or
structure* exists and we are interested in finding it. Let's precise that, based
on the assumptions we make on the model, we can obtain different results. For
example we may be interested in build a Bayesian network that is _markovian_ and
_faithful_ to the distribution, or maybe a causal model that also have the
_sufficiency_ property; different choices of assumptions result in different
results and different algorithms to build the wanted structure.

A fundamental concept in structure learning is *Markov equivalence class (MEC)*
that let us define a set of possible graphs (DAGs) that are equivalent; in fact
usually structure learning algorithms returns a MEC and not a single graph.

#important(title: [Markov Equivalence Class])[
  A *Markov equivalence class* is a set of DAGs encoding the same set of
  conditional independences.

  Two DAGs are *Markov equivalent* if and only if they have the same *skeleton*
  and same set of colliders (_v-structures_).
]

The _skeleton_ of a DAG is basically the DAG itself without edges. MECs can be
encoded by *partially completed DAGs (CPDAG)*, that are DAGs where edges that
differs in the MEC, between different DAGs are undirected.

#figure(
  image("images/MEC.png", width: 40%),
  caption: [ Markov Equivalence Class and its CPDAG ],
)

Of course there are ways in which is possible to reduce the obtained MEC, so
that more edges are directed and we don't need to choose among different
combinations.

= Constraint-Based

The first approach to structure learning is the *constraint-based*, that uses
*conditional independence tests* to reconstruct the Markov equivalence class, by
assuming _markov property_, _faithfulness_ and _sufficiency_.

== SGS

One of the main algorithms is the *SGS*, that says that

- Two variables $X$ and $Y$ are adjacent in the skeleton if they are always
  conditionally dependent, so there is no separating set without $X$ and $Y$.
- If $X$ and $Y$ are not adjacent in the skeleton, then exists a third variable
  $W$ that
  - It's adjacent to both $X$ and $Y$
  - Is not a member of any separating set
  that basically means that $W$ is a collider.

The first part of the algorithm finds the skeleton starting from a fully
connected and undirected graph and, by performing conditional independence
tests, removes edges among nodes that are conditionally independent given a
separating set.

Assuming our dataset was generated by the DAG on the left (that we don't have)
and we need, starting from the graph on the right, we have to reconstruct the
skeleton.

