      node(B, [$B$])
      node(C, [$C$])
      node(D, [$D$])

      edge(A, "<|-", B)
      edge(A, "<|-", C)
      edge(B, "-|>", D)
      edge(C, "-|>", D)
    },
  ),
  caption: [ Not Possible DAG ],
)

Other rules exploit the fact the a DAG, by definition, cannot have cycles.

== PC

The *PC* algorithm basically does the same two iterative steps of SGS but,
instead of considering all the separating sets, it considers separating sets of
increasing size. This has a better time complexity on average.

== Testing Strategy

In order to avoid the super exponential complexity of SGS is fundamental to
choose a convenient ordering of testing.

- *Level-wise*: conditioning sets of increasing size (PC algorithm)
- *Node-wise*: one edge at a time, exhausting all possible conditioning sets.
- *Neighborhood*: choose from the neighborhood of the nodes incident on the edge.

Let's also point out that, if we are interested only in one particular relation
$X -> Y$, is possible to find a subgraph containing $X$ and $Y$, and their
adjustment sets (*local causal discovery*).

== Conclusions on Constraint-Based Methods

The CPDAG produced by those methods is enough to answer probabilistic queries
$P(Y | X)$, by just picking one graph in the MEC.

Instead for interventional and counterfactuals queries we need further knowledge
to orient all edges.

Let's also add that, given the graph, we need to choose the distribution
families or the mechanisms and learn the parameters. So the more are the
variables the more CI tests are needed, but this is kind of independent from the
structure learning method.

= Search and Score

Another possible way to find structures is by *search and score*, a.k.a *model
selection*, where we search in *graph space* $"Graph"(Y)$ of all the possible
graphs that can be built with the set of variables $Y$, score each structure and
choose the best one.

== Score Function

The way to see how well the graph fits data is through a *score function*, that
must satisfy two main properties:

- *Consistency*: same score for graphs in the same equivalence class.
- *Decomposability*: can be locally computed.

And there are two families of approaches for these functions:

- *Information theoretic*: based on data likelihood plus some model complexity
  penalization term.
- *Bayesian*: score the structures using a graph posterior (likelihood and a
  proper prior choice).

A popular choice is the *bayesian information criterion (BIC)*, defined as

$ S(cal(G); D) = log p_hat(theta) (D | cal(G)) - k/2 log n $

with $n$ the number of samples and $k$ the number of parameters (can be the
number of edges). The BIC is *consistent* and *local*.

== Search Strategy

Finding maximal scoring structures is NP-complete so we need a strategy to make
the search feasible:

- *Constrain search strategy*: starting from a candidate structure that is
  iteratively modified by local operations.
- *Constrain search space*: search in some reduced space with interesting properties.

=== GES

The *greedy equivalent search (GES)*  is from the second type of approach, in
fact it searches in the MEC, by performing three different operations:

- *Insertion*: add an edge leading to a different MEC.
- *Deletion*: remove an edge leading to a different MEC.
- *Reversal*: flip an edge leading to a different MEC.

The algorithm greedly tries all possible combinations of those three operations
and, by using the BIC score, the GES ensures to find the MEC of the true DAG.

= Better than MEC

Both methods return a MEC and without prior additional knowledge or
interventions is not possible to do better.

#note(title: [Pearl and Geiger])[
  For heteroskedastic *linear Gaussian* and *multinomial* causal relations, an
  algorithm that identifies the *Markov equivalence class* of the true model is
  *complete*.
]

There are some cases in which we can actually do better, for example with ANM.
